{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Weeks</b> — The Gaon [Saadia] said: these weeks are years, and the proof is that he said afterward: 'until the completion of three weeks of days,' and he did not say with the seventy weeks 'days,' and they are like 'seven sabbaths of years.' And his explanation is correct, for the half-week that he mentioned is one thousand two hundred and ninety days, as I shall explain with complete proof. Only the interpretation of these seventy weeks is extremely difficult. Furthermore: 'to finish the transgression and to end sin' — we do not know whether it is all praise or blame, for from the words 'to atone for iniquity and to anoint the holy of holies' it appears that it is praise, but 'to seal vision and prophet' is not praise. How then can we fix 'to finish transgression and to end sin' to be praise?! And likewise 'to seal vision and prophet.' Now we find written: 'for the iniquity of the Amorite is not yet complete,' and it is blame, the meaning being that until now the day of his calamity has not come, the end of the punishment of his iniquity, like 'great is my iniquity beyond bearing,' and likewise 'if your iniquity is called [such],' and likewise 'your iniquity is ended' — the iniquity is complete and it is blame. And the respondent may answer that 'sin' and 'guilt' [refer to sacrifices]. Furthermore: the word 'please lift' is the opposite — 'and bear her iniquity' — and all is interpreted according to the context of the passage, indicating to bring everlasting righteousness for praise." "<b>And the meaning of 'to seal vision'</b> — that all the prophets prophesied concerning the Second Temple. And now I shall state the words of the Gaon [Saadia]. He said: 'the word went forth' means that He decreed that Jerusalem would be destroyed with the Second Temple after four hundred and ninety years, and they are seventy weeks. But you erred in your reckoning: seventy years were completed, yet they are only seven weeks, which are forty-nine years, and he did not bother to mention the two years because they were two. And he interpreted 'until an anointed prince' — that is King Cyrus — and brought a proof from the words of the prophet: 'Thus said YHWH to His anointed, to Cyrus, whose right hand I have held.' And 'sixty-two weeks' are the days of the Second Temple. Now one may object, for the angel said 'at the beginning of your supplications the word went forth.' Furthermore: how can he intermix the two exiles with the years of the Second Temple, and how do we interpret 'to finish transgression and to end sin'? Furthermore: what do we do with the remaining week, of which he said: 'and he shall confirm a covenant with many for one week' after 'sixty-two weeks,' when it would have been proper to mention, since three weeks still remained? And his proof, that Cyrus is 'anointed,' is not correct, for 'His anointed' means His prophet, as it is written: 'because YHWH has anointed me.'" "<b>And before I state my opinion I shall interpret 'and he shall confirm a covenant with many'</b> — this was well known, for Titus made a covenant with Israel for seven years, and three and a half years before the destruction of the Temple the daily offering was abolished in the Second Temple, and thus it is written in the book of Joseph ben Gurion." "<b>And he said: 'upon the wing of abominations shall be one who makes desolate'</b> — that they placed abominations in the debir after the aforementioned count, when Jerusalem was captured. And it is written in the fourth prophecy: 'and they shall profane the sanctuary, the stronghold' — this is the day Jerusalem was captured in the days of Titus, and the daily offering was removed before this, and the abomination of desolation was set up. And thus it is written: 'and from the time the daily offering is removed and the abomination of desolation is set up — one thousand two hundred and ninety days.' And Daniel needed to explain how many whole days are half the week, because of the two intercalations and also because of the word 'half,' for it is possible that it is not a complete half, less or more, like 'and half the tribe of Manasseh,' and many like this. And know that 'days' are always days and not years. Only it is possible that if he said 'days,' it may be a complete year when the days of the year return, as they were — like 'days as at the beginning,' which are the days of a complete year; 'days' means the day of his redemption, but only when he says with 'days' a number — 'two days' or 'three' — it is not possible for them to be years, but days. As in the interpretation of 'two days' that were two complete years, when the days returned as they were, and likewise 'for a month of days' until you see the moon in the arrangement it appeared on the first day of the count. Therefore I said that one thousand two hundred and ninety days are the half-week that he mentioned, and thus 'happy is he who waits and reaches the days,' as I shall explain. And if they were years, how could a person wait a thousand years and reach them, when it is written: 'the days of our years among them are seventy years'?! Now we find Nehemiah said: 'the city, the house of my fathers' graves, lies in ruins,' and it is written: 'he also commanded me to be governor in the land of Judah,' and it is written concerning him: 'he shall build the temple of YHWH and he shall bear splendor and sit and rule on his throne,' and Jeremiah prophesied concerning him, and he reigned as king and prospered. And in Ezra it is written concerning Nehemiah: 'and you shall be to them for a king.'" "<b>And now I shall interpret 'seventy weeks'</b> — they are from the going forth of the word at the beginning of Daniel's supplications, 'to finish the transgression,' like 'for the iniquity of the Amorite is not yet complete.'" "<b>'And to end sin'</b> — like 'your iniquity is ended.'" "<b>'And to atone for iniquity'</b> — to bear the yoke of the exile." "<b>'To atone for iniquity'</b> — of our fathers, and to bring a time when HaShem will place it in righteousness. Or its interpretation is blame, for the 'coming of righteousness' is its setting, just as the sun's 'coming' is its setting. Therefore it is blame, for as praise it is the opposite, like 'then your righteousness shall go forth as brightness' and 'bring forth your righteousness as light,' and this is 'arise, shine, for your light has come' — for until now your light has set." "<b>'And to seal vision and prophet'</b> — that the prophets shall cease. <b>'And to anoint the holy of holies'</b> — and this is the beginning of the exile. And let not the matter of the reckoning arise in your heart, for in the reckoning of the king of Persia the conjunction adds according to the word of the angel, as I shall explain. And if there is addition or subtraction in the reckoning it does no harm. Perhaps the matter of the conjunction will arise in your heart, for one who knows the moment of the lunar darkness in this year and finds another darkness before it a hundred years earlier — can he thereby know the true position of the moon? According to his reckoning it will return in his will at the latter end, or the coming years. And this is the interpretation: 'and you shall know and understand from the going forth of the word to restore and rebuild Jerusalem.'"], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {8 "I read המולד as 'the conjunction' (i.e., lunar conjunction/molad); the word appears corrupt in context but the astronomical discussion of lunar position supports this reading."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:21:07.304071858Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}